\DOC new_coinductive_definition

\TYPE {new_coinductive_definition : term -> thm * thm * thm}

\SYNOPSIS
Define a relation or family of relations coinductively.

\DESCRIBE
The function {new_coinductive_definition} is applied to a conjunction of
``rules'' of exactly the same form as for {new_inductive_definition}, namely
{!x1...xn. Pi ==> Ri t1 ... tk}, but this conjunction is interpreted as a
coinductive definition of the relations {Ri}. That is, instead of the
smallest relations closed under the rules, the relations are defined to be the
largest ones consistent with the rules, in the sense that each tuple
satisfying one of them arises by one of the rules from tuples satisfying them.
As with {new_inductive_definition}, explicit definitions are made, a new
constant is defined for each {Ri}, and a triple of theorems is returned. The
first one is the ``rule'' theorem, which essentially matches the input clauses
except that the {Ri} are now the new constants; this says that the new
relations are closed under the rules. The second is a coinduction theorem,
asserting that the relations are the largest ones consistent with the rules:
if some relations {Ri'} are such that each tuple satisfying one of them arises
by one of the rules from tuples satisfying the {Ri'}, then the {Ri'} are
included in the {Ri}. Finally, the cases theorem shows how each tuple
satisfying the relations arises by the rules, in the same form as the
hypothesis of the coinduction theorem.

\FAILURE
Fails if the clauses are malformed, if the constants are already in use, or if
there are unproven monotonicity hypotheses. In the last case, you can try
{prove_coinductive_relations_exist} to examine these hypotheses, and either
try to prove them manually or extend {monotonicity_theorems} to let HOL do it.

\EXAMPLE
A relation {R} has an infinite path from {x} if {x} has an {R}-successor from
which there is an infinite path. Interpreted inductively this rule, having no
base case, would define the empty relation. Interpreted coinductively it gives
the intended notion:
{
  # let infpath_RULES,infpath_COINDUCT,infpath_CASES =
      new_coinductive_definition
       `!x:A y. R x y /\ infpath R y ==> infpath R x`;;
  val infpath_RULES : thm = |- !R x y. R x y /\ infpath R y ==> infpath R x
  val infpath_COINDUCT : thm =
    |- !R infpath'.
           (!a. infpath' a ==> (?y. R a y /\ infpath' y))
           ==> (!a. infpath' a ==> infpath R a)
  val infpath_CASES : thm = |- !R a. infpath R a <=> (?y. R a y /\ infpath R y)
}
\noindent To show that there are infinite paths from all the elements of a
set, it suffices to show that each of them has a successor in the set, and
apply the coinduction theorem. For example, there is an infinite increasing
sequence of numbers starting at any number:
{
  # g `!n. infpath (<) n`;;
  val it : goalstack = 1 subgoal (1 total)

  `!n. infpath (<) n`

  # e(MP_TAC(ISPECL [`(<)`; `\n:num. T`] infpath_COINDUCT) THEN
      REWRITE_TAC[]);;
  val it : goalstack = 1 subgoal (1 total)

  `((!a. ?y. a < y) ==> (!a. infpath (<) a)) ==> (!n. infpath (<) n)`

  # e(MESON_TAC[ARITH_RULE `n < SUC n`]);;
  0..0..solved at 2
  0..0..solved at 2
  val it : goalstack = No subgoals
}
\noindent Mutual coinductive definitions, schematic variables and the use of
{monotonicity_theorems} are all as for {new_inductive_definition}.

\SEEALSO
derive_strong_coinduction, new_coinductive_set, new_inductive_definition,
prove_coinductive_relations_exist, prove_monotonicity_hyps,
the_coinductive_definitions.

\ENDDOC
